---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { INSTRUMENTS, OPEN_COUNT, BASE } from "../data/instruments.js";
---

<Page
  title="Analytical instruments for open research"
  description="A studio that builds the pipelines, models, evaluation and interactive instruments for researchers who already have a finding. The interpretation stays theirs."
  current="/"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Open data · Indonesia · instruments, not dashboards</p>
    <h1>The meaning is yours.<br /><em>The measurement is ours.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A finding in a paper is a static figure and a table. This studio does the whole
      quantitative half — the acquisition pipeline, the models, their evaluation against
      honest baselines, and an interactive instrument — and hands the interpretation back
      to the person who knows the subject.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="cta" href="#instruments">{OPEN_COUNT} instruments seeking an author</a>
      <a class="cta ghost" href="/contribute/">How this works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Why<span>the gap this fills</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>Journals publish figures. Nobody publishes instruments.</h2>
      <p>
        A paper's analysis is usually its most reusable part and its least accessible.
        The figures are flattened into a PDF, the pipeline lives on one laptop, and the
        question a reader actually wants to ask — <em>what happens if I change this
        assumption?</em> — cannot be asked at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Repositories archive the code. Notebook hosts run it. Neither builds the thing a
        non-specialist can use, and no journal will host it for you. That is the gap.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p><b>You have a finding. The studio builds the measurement. The interpretation stays yours.</b></p>
        <p class="dim">
          No claim on your finding, no editorial control over your interpretation, no veto
          on your conclusions. The studio does the quantitative work and says so; the
          finding stays yours.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">How<span>what each side brings</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>The trade, stated plainly</h2>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>The studio does</th><th>You bring</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Method.</b> Which estimator, which baseline, what would count as
                working — decided and written down before anything runs</td>
              <td><b>The question,</b> and why it matters in your field</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Models.</b> Built, tuned and scored — statistical or machine-learned,
                whichever the problem actually needs</td>
              <td><b>Domain knowledge.</b> What a result means, what would be surprising,
                what the literature already settled</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Evaluation.</b> Against naive rivals that already contain the easy
                knowledge, with every gate published including the failures</td>
              <td><b>Plausible mechanism.</b> Why the relationship would exist, or why it
                would not</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>The instrument.</b> Pipeline, dashboard, figures, all re-runnable</td>
              <td><b>The interpretation,</b> the written discussion, and accountability for
                the finding</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Credit is recorded with <a href="/contribute/#credit">CRediT roles</a>, so nobody
        has to pretend. You lead: Conceptualization, Investigation, Writing. The studio
        takes the quantitative roles — Methodology, Formal analysis, Validation, Software,
        Data curation, Visualization. What the studio does not have is the one thing that
        makes a case worth doing: knowledge of your subject.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Both sides<span>read this before deciding</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>What you get, and what you should know</h2>
      <div class="sides">
        <div class="side good">
          <h3>What you get</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A <b>working instrument</b> for your finding, built for you, free</li>
            <li><b>First author.</b> The research is yours; the studio is methods
              co-author</li>
            <li>You <b>keep copyright</b> and can republish anywhere</li>
            <li>A <b>DOI</b> if you want one — archived, citable, versioned</li>
            <li>Thresholds fixed before results, so the evaluation is defensible</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="side warn">
          <h3>What you should know</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Not peer reviewed.</b> Gates are checking, not refereeing</li>
            <li>This site has <b>no audience yet</b>. You are betting it gets one</li>
            <li>Publishing here first makes it a <b>preprint</b> — check your journal</li>
            <li><b>Failed gates are published.</b> If the instrument does not support your
              finding, the page says so</li>
            <li>Pipelines are <b>built with AI assistance</b>, disclosed on every case</li>
            <li>It runs on one person's time and money. <b>It could stop.</b></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <p>
          The fourth item on the right is the one to be sure about before starting.
          Validation thresholds are set before any result is seen and never moved to fit
          an outcome. If the instrument contradicts your finding, that is what gets
          published — and it is the whole reason the rest of it is worth believing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="dim">
        Longer versions: <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a> ·
        <a href="/publishing/">publishing and DOIs</a> ·
        <a href="/rights/">rights and licences</a> ·
        <a href="/standards/">the standard every instrument meets</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="instruments">
    <div class="rail">Instruments<span>{OPEN_COUNT} seeking an author</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>Ten instruments, each missing one thing</h2>
      <p>
        Every one is complete as engineering: the pipeline runs, the gates are published
        including the failures, the dashboard works. What none of them has is an
        interpretation — because that is not the studio's to write.
      </p>

      <ul class="cards">
        {INSTRUMENTS.map((i) => (
          <li class="card">
            <div class="card-head">
              <a class="card-title" href={`${BASE}/${i.slug}/`}>
                {i.title} <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              </a>
              <span class="chip">seeking author</span>
            </div>
            <p class="card-line">{i.line}</p>
            <p class="card-found"><b>What it found.</b> {i.found}</p>
            <p class="card-gap"><b>What is missing.</b> {i.gap}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          If one of these is your subject, the work is already done except for the part
          only you can do. Write the interpretation and take <b>first author</b>; the studio
          is <b>methods co-author</b> for the analysis it already built.
        </p>
        <p class="dim">Read <a href="/contribute/">how to contribute</a> first — including the parts that might put you off.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="rail">Engineers<span>a smaller ask</span></div>
    <div class="col">
      <h2>You do not have to build one to take part</h2>
      <p>
        The most useful contribution an engineer can make here takes a day, and it is the
        one this site depends on most.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Contribution</th><th>Effort</th><th>Credit</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Break it.</b> Attack the evaluation. If you succeed, that gets published</td>
              <td>a day</td><td>Validation</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Beat the baseline.</b> Build a stronger rival than the one it was scored against</td>
              <td>a week</td><td>Formal analysis</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><b>Build one.</b> A whole instrument for a researcher's finding</td>
              <td>weeks</td><td>Software, Visualization</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Three specific openings, today: <b>transit-equity</b> has two failing hard gates —
        are they fatal or cosmetic? <b>rice-security</b>'s R² goes −11.09 to 0.82 through
        OLS calibration — is that legitimate? <b>fire-haze</b> uses ROC-AUC on a 3.8% event
        class — is that the right metric?
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent); margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 26ch; }
  .hero-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 1.8rem; }
  .cta {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.82rem; font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em; text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.05rem; border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground); background: var(--accent);
  }
  .cta.ghost { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); }
  .cta { box-shadow: 0 0 28px -10px var(--glow); }
  .cta:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 88%, var(--ink)); color: var(--ground);
    box-shadow: 0 0 34px -6px var(--glow);
  }
  .cta.ghost:hover { background: var(--panel); color: var(--accent); }

  .sec .col.wide { max-width: none; }

  .cards { list-style: none; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 0; }
  .card { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding: 1.4rem 0; }
  .card:first-child { border-top: 1px solid var(--ink); }
  .card-head {
    display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.9rem;
    flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .card-title {
    font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.18rem;
    text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink);
  }
  .card-title:hover { color: var(--accent); text-shadow: 0 0 22px var(--glow); }
  .card-title .arrow { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .card-title:hover .arrow { color: var(--accent); }
  .chip {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.64rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--flag); border: 1px solid currentColor;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.45rem; white-space: nowrap;
    text-shadow: 0 0 14px var(--glow-flag);
    box-shadow: 0 0 18px -12px var(--glow-flag);
  }
  .card-line { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: 62ch; }
  .card-found, .card-gap {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: 74ch;
  }
  .card-found b, .card-gap b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .card-gap { color: var(--ink); }
  .card-gap b { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
